re and  from there i can go here here is the  second or third decision do i switch  here because here there are buses that  go straight to work sometimes  but it might be faster to continue here  and then arrive  here at the central station so the  central station has three different  stops that has different options and  different trams different buses so you  can either be here or you can be here  and sometimes you'll come with another  bus  and go here and then you have to decide  do i go here for the fastest bus to work  or do i go here this aggregates to me  having to google all these different  stops leading to so many searches in the  app that it drives me crazy every  morning so if i could do this with just  opening my own app clicking once on a  pre-determined search that aggregates  all these together  would make my life a lot better  so hopefully you understand the benefit  i could get from this app okay let's see  if we can get some api access here i'll  just do a  random search and we see we have three  requests the first one i recognize  that's just a google analytics one then  we have a request to api slash token  slash public slash new returns a token  then we have journeys we have links and  we have results let's see we have a few  results here we have one two three we  have six results in the list and we have  six results here so this seems very  interesting so we have is departed and  then we have trip legs trip leg has a  bunch of information here we have  something called line seems very  interesting as well planned arrival time  i think  this is what we're looking for in the  api so a good thing to try would be to  open this in a new tab essentially going  with the browser towards the api so here  we see a problem we have missing  credentials make sure your api  invocation call has header  authorization bearer access token but we  did see this request we have a token  here including  615e  so we can check if  that header is present here then  request headers  authorization bearer  and i do recognize  this token hash being the one that is  being delivered here so  hopefully if we just use this token  we will have programmatic access another  thing i noticed was that in the request  we have  a bunch of query parameters and they use  for origin gid and destination gid it's  some form of id for i'm guessing it's  these two  origin destination so probably i have to  dig out these ids connect them to  the names  that i'm interested in and have a  mapping in the front and between  the name and this id so i think we  should try it in insomnia we we try it  with this token just to make sure we  have proper access okay so we're now in  insomnia we can see that we get the  missing credentials  but if i take the token that i just had  in the browser we do get a result here  so that's great the api access is solved  okay so i created this configuration  file for the app that we're going to  build so first i dug out the ids for a  bunch of different bus and tram stops  here that i'm interested in i made this  configuration called decisions that maps  a name of what the decision is so this  is going from home to work so i have  three different options of  of getting on  public transport  three different stops  and the destination is work  so my plan is that it will be three  requests  all receiving different departures and  aggregate them in the same list of  departures and sort them on departure  time so i can get on a bus or tram as  quickly as possible these decisions will  become like buttons on a home screen so  i can just click  on one of the decisions that i need to  make and then  quickly get information on where to go  so now i have created a next api route  that works as a middle layer between the  front end and the the api that we looked  at in the beginning so from my front end  i pass to the next api in the query  parameters origin id destination id and  of course the public token that we need  to have to access the api so i build up  the api string the the url with the  query parameters we execute the request  and then we filter those results that  comes back because we don't want results  that where the the bus or tram is  already departed so we filter those out  we make sure that there are at least  one trip leg  and we make sure that of the trip legs  that we receive  none of them are cancelled or partly  cancelled because then it doesn't make  sense to to show it and then just return  the results and i plan to use  react query because in react query  they have dependent queries  so i would first need to make a request  to get the token  and calling this api endpoint will have  a dependency on that the token is  fetched first and then we can use this  one  so i will use react queries dependent  queries and then of course  we need to also  make many requests  and aggregate them together and react  query has functionality for that too  so that you can ex they have a they have  a hook called use queries  and so i'm going to code that together  and then i'm going to  go through that code as well okay so  let's take a look at how the front end  will fetch this data because i think use  i think react query can really help out  in simplifying a lot of this  so we use the react query use query hook  and we call this query token  and it's just for fetching the token  and we need to have a token in order to  execute the  the api for getting different routes  so if we look at  the route queries this enabled prop here  that makes sure that we have got a token  before we execute this query  because we need to pass that token to  the query  and this is not using the use query hook  this is using use queries because it's  going to be multiple we aggregate  multiple requests together into a list  of results and they all get  the same query key so they will be  aggregated in the departures query and  for each we pass in a different origin  id so even though we perform multiple  queries they will all become aggregated  into these departures responses it will  be like a growing array  of finished queries that will populate  the data of this array  so a react query can really help out  when you want to do multiple queries and  aggregate the data together which is  really nice  okay so i am now finishing up the ui  coding and what is cool about this  specific api  is that if you look at my typings of the  api response here  you can see that a drip leg has a line  and the line  has these properties  it has background color and foreground  color  so i can match  the real app how that displays these  lines  by utilizing the background color and  foreground color  essentially what they're set up is that  they from the back inside can control  how the front end is presenting these  lines so if they were to  like rename a certain line and change  the colors of it  and the frontend could update just by  the api changing what colors it sends  down  of course it's a little bit of an over  fetch  to have it like this because  you might do multiple requests and  you're always getting these color  informations on every request but  it might be a good decision you know  so i created this component called  line icon that takes in these colors  from the api  and that's how i got  the display colors of each  different line to work so in order to  make a progressive web app out of this i  added the manifest.json so we described  the theme color that it should be  running full screen we have an icon  and so on i designed an icon that looks  like this  so this is the like logo of the public  transport company  so i use that in the logo design and i  also added the doge  for funds okay so here we have the app  running let's say that i am in town and  i want to go home  then i need to aggregate  four different pick-up points so this is  exactly what i'm looking for with just  one click i can see all the information  and i can decide  how to which one to go to and which bus  or tram to jump on  so this is pretty amazing i think it's  so great that you can with a progressive  web app get like the  native app experience and the quickness  and the convenience of a home screen  icon that you just click and you go into  full screen and it's i just love to have  that power  okay so i think i am becoming done with  this now  i have all the functionality i need for  now  and i've spent around three hours doing  this  and if i can save  around five minutes a day by using my  own app instead  let's see three hours would mean 180  minutes divided by 5  would be  around  40  40  35 maybe something like that  so if i use this  every day  for 35 or so work days i could recoup my  three hour development time  and i'm happy with that because usually  when i build stuff it takes me too long  so it will take me like a year to regain  my time  and so i think this was a successful  automation project where i can actually  gain  so much time that i quickly can recoup  it and also of course  the pain is gone from using that other  app that is not optimized for doing  these types of things although it's a  good app in in all other aspects but  you can always  make it even better if you just include  your own specific use cases  um  so i hope you enjoyed watching this  video i hope you got inspired to learn  or inspired to start another hobby  project and see it through and finish it  and if you like this video please  subscribe to the channel thumbs up the  video  share the channel to a friend all those  good things  thank you for watching and i will see  you in the next one  [Music]  [Applause]  you

